# Secrets - never commit
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
*.pfx
*.p12
appsettings.*.local.json

# A developer's own machine: a real server, a real login, a real password. It used to be tracked
# and was safe only because it used Trusted_Connection and named no password - the moment anyone
# pointed it at a SQL login, the password would have been committed. Copy the .example beside it
# and fill it in; that one is tracked and holds placeholders.
appsettings.Development.json

# Same reasoning for the compose override: it points the stack at a developer's own SQL Server and
# carries that login's password, so it is machine-specific and never committed (as in Weir). The
# tracked .example beside it holds placeholders - copy it and fill in your connection.
docker-compose.override.yml

# SQLite sidecar files (write-ahead log and shared memory) - never source.
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Tooling artifacts (Claude Code). Local assistant configuration, not project source:
# it is regenerated per machine and carries no decision anyone reviews, so it stays out of the
# repository as it does in Flare and Weir. CLAUDE.md belongs here for the same reason and one
# more: it records what this particular machine can reach - a proxy host, which databases are
# installed, where their binaries live - which is noise to a reader and nobody else's business.
.claude/
.mcp.json
CLAUDE.md

# User secrets and local settings.
secrets.json
*.userprefs

# Build output for other architectures, and dependencies of any tooling that lands here.
